@title Tutorial - drive the car
@game  PCSA00027
@shot-every 60

# ============================================================================
# THE CONTROL SCHEME. Read this before touching the inputs - it has been got
# wrong twice, in both directions.
#
#   RIGHT stick UP    (ry=0)   accelerate
#   RIGHT stick DOWN  (ry=255) brake / reverse
#   LEFT  stick L/R   (lx=)    steer - a TURN RATE, and `ly` is NOT read
#
# `lx=128` drives straight, `lx>128` turns one way and `lx<128` the other, and
# the rate grows with deflection. Measured over 90 frames at full throttle:
# lx=218 turned about -90 degrees, lx=255 about -254, lx=38 about +80, lx=1
# about +267.
#
# For a while this file claimed the left stick was an ABSOLUTE camera-relative
# heading needing lx and ly set together, because the legend says "ROTATE IN
# DIRECTION". That is REFUTED, by an 8-bearing `explore` sweep from one
# identical state at f1810 with every variant reporting `locate --id <car>`:
# lang=0 (lx=128 ly=1) and lang=180 (lx=128 ly=255) produced BIT-IDENTICAL
# determinism signatures. If the stick were a heading those would be the two
# most different outcomes possible. Every variant grouped strictly by `lx`.
#
# The wrong answer came from holding single inputs and watching by eye. The park
# is small and the car crosses it in about two seconds at full throttle, so a
# long hold always ends against a wall and every reading is contaminated by the
# collision. Settle control questions with a parallel sweep reporting a MEASURED
# quantity, not by driving a session and looking at it.
#
# The in-game legend renders these as three round glyphs labelled L, R, R. They
# are the two STICKS, not the shoulder triggers: `l` and `r` were tested at
# length and do nothing on this screen.
#
# FRAME NUMBERS ARE ON THE CORRECTED CLOCK (a frame is a display-queue flip and
# nothing else). Anything recorded before 2026-07-25 counted voluntary yields
# too and ran ~80x fast, so older landmark frames do not apply.
# ============================================================================

# --- the prefix: identical to gameplay-prefix.recipe -------------------------
# Kept inline rather than included (the format has no include) so this file
# replays standalone. If the boot timings change, change both.
0: @section boot

800: @section accept-terms
820: cross              # ONLINE TERMS dialog is live and scrolling by f800
832:

1250: @section dialogs
1250: cross             # "Welcome to the ... tutorial"
1262:
1400: cross             # "There are 2 control schemes available..."
1412:
1550: cross
1562:
# By f1800 the tutorial shows "Follow the trail to try out the new handling"
# with the REGULAR CONTROLS legend up, and the car is under player control.

# --- drive -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every press is held for many frames on purpose: the guest samples the pad once
# per DISPLAY FRAME, so a short press can fall between two samples and is then
# never observed at all.
1810: @section drive
1810: ry=0              # accelerate away from the start pad
1930: @shot drive-accelerated

1930: ry=0 lx=70        # accelerate + steer left
1990: ry=0              # straighten up
2110: @shot drive-crossed-ground

# The car will eventually meet a wall - the park is enclosed. Reverse out with
# the right stick DOWN, steer while reversing, then drive off in the new
# direction. This is the manoeuvre the rest of the tutorial needs.
2110: @section recover
2110: ry=255            # reverse
2200: ry=255 lx=40      # reverse while turning
2260:                   # settle, so the reverse does not carry into the launch
2280: ry=0 lx=40        # drive away on the new heading
2370: ry=0
2460: @shot drive-recovered

2460: @todo Follow the actual tutorial trail. This recipe just drives around: it
2460: @todo proves control works, not that the tutorial objective completes.
